Fixture design notes
--------------------
* Each rejecting fixture is ONE single-point mutation of the same base
  contract the structural pass already proves is accepted at exit 0, expanded
  through New-StructuralFixtureJson -- the expander T-003 already authored --
  so a rejection cannot be attributed to anything but the value under test.
* Each is PAIRED with an otherwise byte-identical control carrying no
  newline, which must still be ACCEPTED at exit 0. Those controls are the
  non-vacuity guard: the block cannot pass by rejecting everything. The two
  bodies of a pair differ by exactly 2 characters, asserted directly.
* The trailing newline is carried as the two-character JSON escape \n inside
  the string value, NEVER as a raw 0x0A byte. A raw control byte inside a
  JSON string is invalid JSON and would be rejected at the PARSE step, which
  would exercise V2-PARSE instead of the anchors. This was confirmed at the
  byte level before the tests were written (0 raw 0x0A bytes, 1 backslash-n
  sequence per fixture) and is now pinned by two corpus-integrity checks: one
  asserts no byte below 0x20 is present, the other decodes the fixture and
  asserts the field value ends in a real newline character.
* Fixtures are ephemeral (DD-5, INV-006): each is written to a per-test
  temporary file removed again in a finally block. No permanent fixture
  directory is added, and no fixture FILE NAME contains a byte in 0x00-0x1F.
* concepts[0].id retargets CONTEXTS to CONTEXTS_PLAIN for BOTH members of its
  pair, so mutating the id cannot also dangle the terms[].concept_id link and
  report a second, unrelated violation. This mirrors the existing AC-018 (1)
  fixture.
* No credential, token, personal, or customer-derived string appears in any
  fixture; all vocabulary is the synthetic Purchase / Fulfillment domain
  nouns the surrounding suite already uses (security-spec.md).

Observed twin output for the three rejecting fixtures (byte-identical across
both twins, one V2-PATTERN line each; the control character is folded to a
space by each twin's message sanitiser, so the one-violation-per-line
contract holds):

  V2-PATTERN: concepts[0].id: value CONCEPT-ORDER  does not match ^CONCEPT-[A-Z][A-Z0-9-]*$
  V2-PATTERN: concepts[0].name: value Order  does not match ^[A-Z][A-Za-z0-9]*$
  V2-PATTERN: concepts[0].context: value order-taking  does not match ^[a-z][a-z0-9]*(-[a-z0-9]+)*$

Note the message still quotes the DECLARED ^ ... $ source text back to the
author -- that is the schema's own ECMA-262 text and is correct. Only the
COMPILED form uses \A ... \Z / \A ... \z. The new checks assert both halves:
the field-path prefix and the declared-pattern tail.

